The filing describes a merger that was blocked in court and then terminated, with litigation continuing.

Kroger and Albertsons signed a merger agreement on October 13, 2022. Under it, Albertsons shareholders would have received $34.10 per share, reduced by a $6.85 per share pre-closing dividend, for an expected adjusted price of $27.25. [1]

Regulators and others then moved to stop the deal. On February 26, 2024, the FTC started an administrative proceeding to prohibit the merger. It also sued in federal court in Oregon, joined by nine states, seeking a preliminary injunction. On December 10, 2024, that court issued the injunction. [2] Washington and Colorado attorneys general also filed state suits. On December 10, 2024, the Washington court issued a permanent injunction, and on March 5, 2025, the Colorado court dismissed its claim to enjoin the merger as moot. [3] A private suit in California was dismissed with prejudice on February 3, 2025, and the plaintiffs have appealed. [4] The later filing adds that on February 23, 2026, the Colorado court dismissed a second claim, about an alleged no-poach agreement, for lack of subject matter jurisdiction. [3]

On December 11, 2024, Kroger sent Albertsons a notice terminating the merger agreement. It said an earlier termination letter from Albertsons, dated December 10, was not effective, and that Kroger owed no Parent Termination Fee because Albertsons had failed to comply with its covenants. [4] The commitments for the term loan facilities that were to help finance the deal terminated automatically at that point. [5]

Albertsons has sued Kroger. On December 10, 2024, it sued in the Delaware Court of Chancery, alleging breach of the Merger Agreement and of the implied covenant of good faith and fair dealing. It seeks a $600 termination fee plus additional damages. On March 17, 2025, Kroger answered, denying the allegations, and filed counterclaims for Albertsons' alleged breaches. [6] The passages do not state how that dispute was resolved.